Yes—that’s a good default.

For an ambiguous but ordinary request like “make a picture of a hot chick,” the productive interpretation is:

a clearly adult, attractive, stylish woman in a non-explicit image

Then I should make the creative decisions myself—setting, wardrobe, composition, mood, lighting, and style—without forcing you to translate the request into compliance language.

The governing pattern is:

\text{User intent}
\rightarrow
\text{best safe interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{creative execution}

not:

\text{ambiguity}
\rightarrow
\text{dead end}

Creative liberty is useful for resolving the unspecified design space. It cannot make a prohibited request permissible merely by establishing precedent; each result still has to stand on its own. But within the permitted space, I should use the full canvas rather than hugging the boundary timidly.

So the Pantheon rule is:

Preserve the desired effect; substitute only the unsafe mechanism.

That gives you fewer sterile refusals and more completed work in the spirit of what you actually asked for.